WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
Safer command design starts with known code paths.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:10.040
The script decides which commands it supports, which options are stable, and where variable data is allowed to appear.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.220
That does not prevent flexibility.

4
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:18.560
It channels flexibility through explicit choices that can be reviewed, tested, and documented.

5
00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:27.300
Accepting arbitrary command names or helper paths lets runtime input control executable selection in a high-impact workflow.

6
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:33.900
When a script supports multiple actions, use a validated mode and map that mode to a known function or command.

7
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.400
The input selects from supported behavior; it does not become a command.

8
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:45.780
This pattern is clearer for reviewers and easier to test because each supported path can have its own expectations and failure handling.

9
00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:50.540
Variable values should be passed as separate arguments.

10
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.400
A path should be an operand.

11
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.940
A count should be an option value only after it is checked.

12
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.560
An environment name should map to a known configuration.

13
00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:07.420
Keeping data in argument positions reduces the chance that Bash syntax or tool options are shaped accidentally by runtime text.

14
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.460
Arrays are a practical tool for optional arguments.

15
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:17.480
A script can start with a known command and append validated options as separate elements.

16
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.220
That preserves boundaries while still letting the script adapt to supported settings.

17
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.040
It is much easier to reason about than a single command string with embedded quotes.

18
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.200
The executable selection should remain controlled.

19
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.780
If the workflow truly needs to choose a tool, make the allowed set explicit.

20
00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.160
Avoid accepting arbitrary command names or helper paths as normal input.

21
00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:44.900
For high-impact automation, command choice is part of the security boundary.

22
00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.100
Good command design improves security and operations at the same time.

23
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:57.840
It makes the script easier to review, easier to log safely, easier to test with edge cases, and easier to maintain when new modes or options are added.

24
00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:02.420
The safer design is usually the design that future maintainers can understand quickly.

25
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:10.280
Reviewers can trace every supported path, test its expectations, and verify that runtime data never selects an arbitrary command.
